From 9700b31b25ab662788102226fe7770fb3143b271 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "crychic.tan" <crychic.tan@cybertan.com.tw>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 10:49:32 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] generic: mtk_ppe: only clear the expired L2 subflow

With WED enabled on the SonicFi RAP630W-211G, bridger offloads the
bridged LAN <-> WLAN traffic as L2 flows. Only the first one or two
connections between two hosts were offloaded, though. Later ones ran on
the CPU until bridger installed the flow rule again. Connections that
were offloaded at that time kept forwarding in hardware, uncounted and
out of reach of the flow rule.

The pending patch "net: ethernet: mtk_eth_soc: improve keeping track of
offloaded flows" made mtk_flow_entry_update_l2() clear the L2 flow
instead of the subflow that has expired. The first subflow that expires
then removes the L2 flow from the PPE flow table and frees all of its
subflows without invalidating their hardware entries.

Add a pending kernel patch that only clears the expired subflow, as
mainline still does.

Signed-off-by: crychic.tan <crychic.tan@cybertan.com.tw>
---
 ...pe-only-clear-the-expired-L2-subflow.patch | 45 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 45 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 target/linux/generic/pending-6.12/736-06-net-ethernet-mtk_ppe-only-clear-the-expired-L2-subflow.patch

diff --git a/target/linux/generic/pending-6.12/736-06-net-ethernet-mtk_ppe-only-clear-the-expired-L2-subflow.patch b/target/linux/generic/pending-6.12/736-06-net-ethernet-mtk_ppe-only-clear-the-expired-L2-subflow.patch
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..f20fd35b63
--- /dev/null
+++ b/target/linux/generic/pending-6.12/736-06-net-ethernet-mtk_ppe-only-clear-the-expired-L2-subflow.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,45 @@
+From: crychic.tan <crychic.tan@cybertan.com.tw>
+Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: mtk_ppe: only clear the expired L2 subflow
+
+mtk_flow_entry_update_l2() walks the subflows of an L2 flow and drops
+the ones whose hardware entry is no longer bound. Since "net: ethernet:
+mtk_eth_soc: improve keeping track of offloaded flows" it passes the L2
+flow instead of the expired subflow to __mtk_foe_entry_clear(), so the
+first subflow of an L2 flow that expires takes the whole L2 flow with
+it:
+
+- The L2 flow is removed from ppe->l2_flows. The packets of new
+  connections between the same hosts no longer find it in
+  __mtk_ppe_check_skb() and are not offloaded until the flow rule is
+  installed again.
+- All other subflows are freed without invalidating their hardware
+  entries. Those keep forwarding without being counted, and they are
+  not invalidated when the flow rule is removed.
+- The walk goes on with a subflow that has just been freed.
+
+Only clear the subflow that has expired, as the code did before.
+
+On MT7981 with WED, only the first one or two of a series of bridged
+TCP transfers between the same two hosts were offloaded. From the third
+one on they ran on the CPU (AP CPU load 50-58% instead of 6-9%). After
+the flow rule was removed, the hardware entry of an earlier connection
+kept forwarding. With this, six back-to-back transfers were all
+offloaded and counted, and the hardware entries were invalidated along
+with the flow rule.
+
+Signed-off-by: crychic.tan <crychic.tan@cybertan.com.tw>
+---
+ drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_ppe.c |    2 +-
+ 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)
+
+--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_ppe.c
++++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_ppe.c
+@@ -618,7 +618,7 @@ mtk_flow_entry_update_l2(struct mtk_ppe
+ 		if (!mtk_flow_entry_update(ppe, cur, packets, bytes)) {
+ 			entry->prev_packets += cur->prev_packets;
+ 			entry->prev_bytes += cur->prev_bytes;
+-			__mtk_foe_entry_clear(ppe, entry, false);
++			__mtk_foe_entry_clear(ppe, cur, false);
+ 			continue;
+ 		}
+ 
-- 
